::PC_U02_L07_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U02_L07_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.2
Question:
Which feature is most useful when sketching a higher-degree polynomial?
{
=A. Zeros and end behavior#Correct. Zeros and end behavior support polynomial graphing.
~B. Denominator restrictions#This belongs to exponential models.
~C. Growth factor#This belongs to logarithmic models.
~D. Logarithm base#
}
::PC_U02_L07_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U02_L07_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.5
Question:
Which model requires checking where the denominator equals 0?
{
~A. Polynomial model#Polynomial models do not have denominator restrictions unless written rationally.
=B. Rational model#Correct. Rational models require denominator restrictions.
~C. Exponential growth model#Logarithmic models require positive arguments.
~D. Logarithmic model#
}
::PC_U02_L07_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U02_L07_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.1
Question:
A quantity increases by 6% each year. Which model type is most appropriate?
{
~A. Rational#Rational models compare quantities through division.
~B. Polynomial only#Polynomial models do not represent constant percent growth as directly.
=C. Exponential#Correct. Repeated percent growth is exponential.
~D. Logarithmic only#
}
::PC_U02_L07_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U02_L07_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.EX.2
Question:
Which restriction is required for log base 2 of (x - 4)?
{
~A. x < 4#This makes the argument negative.
~B. x = 4#At x = 4 the argument is 0, which is not allowed.
~C. all real numbers#Logarithmic arguments must be positive, so not all real numbers work.
=D. x > 4#Correct. The argument x - 4 must be positive, so x > 4.
}
::PC_U02_L07_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U02_L07_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.FA.3
Question:
Why is context important in a polynomial model?
{
=A. It can restrict valid inputs and outputs.#Correct. Context determines which algebraic values make sense.
~B. It removes all zeros.#Some inputs may not make sense in context.
~C. It makes every input valid.#Context does not change the function family.
~D. It changes every polynomial into a logarithm.#
}